course: dictionary
lesson: 7704
title: The prefix pseudo-
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A prefix is a small piece added to the front of a word to change its meaning. «Pseudo-» is a prefix that means false or pretend. It tells you that something looks like something it is not.
explain: «His pseudo-intellectual talk annoyed everyone.» Pseudo-intellectual means pretending to be clever. The clue is the pseudo- at the front, so ask yourself: does the talk sound clever but is really fake? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Scientists and writers use pseudo- in school words too. A pseudonym is a pen name, a made-up name used instead of your real one. Pseudoscience looks like science but does not use proper evidence, like horoscopes. Pseudocode is a plan for a program written in simple words.
fact: The prefix «pseudo-» means false or pretend.
fact: A pseudonym is a pen name, like Mark Twain, which was not his real name.
fact: Pseudoscience looks like science but does not use proper evidence, such as horoscopes.
fact: Pseudocode is a plan for a computer program written in simple words.
fact: «Pseudo-random» numbers look random but come from a starting number called a seed.
exercise: «His pseudo-intellectual talk annoyed everyone.» What does pseudo- mean here? | Clever and kind | Very loud and fast | *Fake, pretending to be clever | Brand new
exercise: Mark Twain was not his real name. What is a name like that called? | *A pseudonym | A synonym | A seed | A pseudo-code
exercise: What does the prefix «pseudo-» mean? | Very fast | Two of something | *False or pretend | Very old
test: «Most games make random numbers with a pseudo-random number generator.» What are pseudo-random numbers? | *Numbers that look random but come from a starting number | Numbers that are always even | Numbers chosen by a teacher | Numbers that are truly random with no pattern at all
test: What is pseudocode? | A type of horoscope | *A plan for a program written in simple words | A fake computer that does not work | A very long number
exam: Which one is pseudoscience? | Testing a plant with a fair experiment | *A horoscope that claims to predict your day | Counting the stars with a telescope | Measuring how fast a ball rolls
